          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "At what blood pressure reading is hypertension diagnosed, and do the major bodies agree?",
          "answer": "**Short answer:** The guidance I could retrieve gives a clear threshold for US guidance and a different one for European guidance. It does not give NICE or WHO thresholds, so I can't tell you whether all four agree.\n\n**US cardiology guidance (the source describes it as \"United States society guidance\"; the retrieved text doesn't name the specific society):** Office readings are classified as follows. The higher category applies when systolic and diastolic fall into different ones.\n- Normal: top number below 120 and bottom number below 80.\n- Elevated: top number 120 to 129, bottom number below 80.\n- Stage 1 hypertension: top number 130 to 139, or bottom number 80 to 89.\n- Stage 2 hypertension: top number at least 140, or bottom number at least 90.\n\n*Source: Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets.*\n\n**European guidance:** The same source says European guidance describes office hypertension as beginning at 140/90 mm Hg. It treats the 130 to 139 / 80 to 89 band as \"high-normal\" rather than as stage 1 disease. So the same reading can be labeled differently depending on which body's guidance is used. The source notes that this changes how many people are counted as hypertensive.\n\n**Where they differ:** The retrieved guidance documents a disagreement between US and European thresholds. It also says the two differ in how aggressively they treat low-risk stage 1 disease. The source describes lifestyle advice alone for many people the US calls stage 1, but it does not give the full European approach.\n\n**Where they agree:** The retrieved passages don't document any agreement between the bodies on the diagnostic threshold.\n\n**NICE and WHO:** I could not retrieve their thresholds. I have not filled that gap from memory. To get them, check the current NICE hypertension guideline and the WHO hypertension fact sheet directly.\n\n**Evidence strength:** The passages list the thresholds but do not say how strong the evidence behind each one is, so I can't comment on that here.\n\n**Practical point:** A diagnosis rests on repeated measurements over time, not a single reading. One high reading in a clinic is not in itself a diagnosis. A doctor or nurse can explain which threshold applies in your situation and how to confirm a reading.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
